import numpy as np

def rot(theta):
    c, s = np.cos(theta), np.sin(theta)
    return np.array([[c, -s], [s, c]])

def rotate_about(p, c, theta):
    p, c = np.asarray(p, float), np.asarray(c, float)
    return c + rot(theta) @ (p - c)

def undo(theta):
    return rot(theta).T

print(np.round(rotate_about((2, 1), (1, 1), np.pi / 2), 6))
print(np.allclose(undo(0.7) @ rot(0.7), np.eye(2)), np.allclose(undo(0.7), rot(-0.7)))
```

Rotation about a point is *not* a linear map (it moves the origin), which is why robotics adds a third coordinate and uses 3×3 homogeneous matrices for 2D poses. You'll meet that in the coordinate-frames lesson.

